WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:12.440
Your telemetry dashboard needs the total errors between any two minutes, a million times a second, while new errors keep arriving. A Fenwick tree does both fast.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:14.568
Pause and think.

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:18.881
Why is a plain prefix sum array not enough when values change?

00:00:19.281 --> 00:00:26.595
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:35.109
Prefix sums and Fenwick trees answer one question very fast: what is the total between position l and position r?

00:00:35.128 --> 00:00:48.363
New word: Fenwick tree. Binary Indexed Tree: a compact array that answers prefix sums and updates in log time. Picture it like this: A ledger where each page stores subtotals of a power-of-two run.

00:00:48.763 --> 00:00:58.156
New word: prefix sum. An array where each entry is the total of everything before it. Picture it like this: Mile markers showing distance from the start.

00:01:00.678 --> 00:01:02.176
Think of a car odometer.

00:01:02.626 --> 00:01:07.456
To know how far you drove between Tuesday and Friday, you do not re-drive the road.

00:01:07.906 --> 00:01:11.691
You read the odometer on Friday and subtract the reading from Tuesday.

00:01:12.141 --> 00:01:17.818
A prefix sum array is an odometer for an array: each cell stores the running total so far.

00:01:17.828 --> 00:01:26.268
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:01:28.778 --> 00:01:31.042
Here is an array and its prefix array.

00:01:31.492 --> 00:01:36.414
P of i is the sum of the first i numbers, with P of zero equal to zero.

00:01:36.864 --> 00:01:41.357
The sum from index one to index three is P of four minus P of one.

00:01:41.807 --> 00:01:46.301
That is twelve minus three, which is nine, computed with one subtraction.

00:01:47.651 --> 00:01:51.691
Prefix sums plus a hash map count subarrays that add up to k.

00:01:52.141 --> 00:01:56.738
As we walk, we keep a map from each prefix total to how many times we saw it.

00:01:57.188 --> 00:02:03.841
If the current total minus k was seen before, every earlier spot with that total starts a subarray summing to k.

00:02:04.291 --> 00:02:07.240
This turns an n squared search into one pass.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:15.033
But plain prefix sums break when values change: one update forces you to rewrite every later total.

00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:19.884
A Fenwick tree, also called a binary indexed tree, fixes this.

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:25.570
Each cell stores the sum of a block whose length is the lowest set bit of its index.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.569
Update and query both walk about log n cells, by adding or removing that lowest bit.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.462
Here is a query for the first seven numbers.

00:02:35.912 --> 00:02:39.836
Seven is binary one one one. Cell seven covers one number.

00:02:40.286 --> 00:02:43.827
Drop the lowest bit to get six, which covers two numbers.

00:02:44.277 --> 00:02:48.944
Drop again to get four, which covers four numbers. Three cells, and we are done.

00:02:50.294 --> 00:02:58.909
Static data: use a prefix array. Changing data: use a Fenwick tree. Ranges of any shape: say segment tree as the follow up.

00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:00.139
Back to the question.

00:03:00.539 --> 00:03:04.451
Why is a plain prefix sum array not enough when values change?

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:08.764
An update would force rewriting every later prefix, which is O of n.

00:03:09.964 --> 00:03:10.835
Quick recap.

00:03:11.235 --> 00:03:14.079
Prefix sums answer range totals instantly.

00:03:14.479 --> 00:03:17.161
Fenwick trees also allow fast updates.

00:03:17.561 --> 00:03:19.523
Both are O of log n or better.
